Main debugging conclusions so far

1. Early bug: wrong lock released

At one point we released curr->lock without acquiring it.
That was wrong because find_proc_by_pid() only acquires target->lock, not curr->lock.

2. Another bug: releasing target->lock too early

If target was sleeping inside co_sleep() and expected to resume from sched(), releasing target->lock before direct swtch() caused target to later run into panic: release when it resumed and tried to release its own lock.

3. Big insight: phase-1 waiting and phase-2 waiting are not identical

A process sleeping from co_sleep() resumes under different lock assumptions than a process sleeping from a previous direct switch.
This is why we introduced channel tagging.

4. Current likely remaining issue

The remaining crash probably comes from a resume-path mismatch when a coroutine-sleeping process is resumed due to kill() / scheduler behavior rather than normal peer handoff.
That means some path assumes a lock is held when it is not, or assumes a lock is free when it is not.

